---
import SimLayout from '../../../layouts/SimLayout.astro';
import Slider from '../../../components/sim/Slider.astro';
import Checks from '../../../components/sim/Checks.astro';
import Transport from '../../../components/sim/Transport.astro';
import Result from '../../../components/sim/Result.astro';
import Caption from '../../../components/sim/Caption.astro';
import Readout from '../../../components/sim/Readout.astro';
---

<SimLayout
  areaSlug="deep-learning"
  title="Batch Size & Gradient Noise"
  description="Nobody computes the real gradient. A handful of examples is drawn at random, each one pulls in its own direction, and their average is the step. Watch which examples were drawn, watch the arrow they add up to, and watch how much it misses the true gradient by."
  shortcuts={['run', 'step', 'reset', 'fullscreen']}
>
  <div class="sim">
    <div class="sim-view">
      <div class="sim-stage">
        <canvas
          id="bn"
          width="1000"
          height="730"
          role="img"
          aria-label="The 256 examples with the drawn batch lit up, the loss surface with each drawn example's own gradient, the batch average and the true gradient, and a plot of the error against batch size."
        ></canvas>
      </div>
      <Caption
        legend={[
          { label: 'the gradient over all 256 examples', color: '#5eead4' },
          { label: 'the examples you drew, and each one on its own', color: '#a78bfa' },
          { label: 'the average of them, which is the step', color: '#7c8cff' },
          { label: 'where the run has been', color: '#f59e0b' },
        ]}
        hint="Hover an example to see the direction it pulls · click the surface to start a run from there"
      />
      <Result label="This batch against the truth" />
    </div>

    <div class="sim-panel">
      <Transport step>
        <button type="button" class="btn ghost" id="draw">New batch</button>
      </Transport>
      <Slider id="batch" label="Batch size" min={1} max={256} value={8} hint={['one example', 'the whole set']} />
      <Slider id="lr" label="Learning rate" min={1} max={120} value={30} display="0.30" />
      <Checks
        items={[
          { id: 'singles', label: 'Each drawn example pulling on its own', checked: true },
          { id: 'cloud', label: 'The other batches you could have drawn', checked: true },
        ]}
      />
      <Readout
        title="Values"
        rows={[
          { id: 'r-drawn', label: 'examples drawn' },
          { id: 'r-angle', label: 'this batch points off by' },
          { id: 'r-err', label: 'error of this draw' },
          { id: 'r-noise', label: 'typical error of a draw' },
          { id: 'r-true', label: 'size of the true gradient' },
          { id: 'r-ratio', label: 'noise ÷ signal' },
          { id: 'r-ball', label: 'radius it settles into' },
          { id: 'r-steps', label: 'steps taken' },
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </div>

  <Fragment slot="observe">
    <li>
      The left panel is the whole training set, one dot per example. Press
      <b>New batch</b> and a handful of them light up, no example twice, the
      way an epoch shuffles the set and deals it out. That draw, and nothing
      else, is what the next step is allowed to know about.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each lit example gets its own violet arrow on the surface, and they point
      all over the place. An example's arrow runs along the line from the origin
      to its dot in the data panel, because a linear model's gradient for one
      example is its own features scaled by how wrong the prediction is. Hover a
      dot to see its arrow pick itself out.
    </li>
    <li>
      The indigo arrow is the average of the violet ones, and it is the step
      that actually gets taken. The teal arrow is the gradient over all 256
      examples, the direction nobody can afford to compute. The gap between the
      two is the whole subject.
    </li>
    <li>
      Hold still and press <b>New batch</b> a few times. The indigo arrow jumps
      around while the teal one stays put, and each draw drops a dot on the
      bottom plot. The dots scatter around the curve: that curve is the typical
      miss, not a promise about any single draw.
    </li>
    <li>
      At <b>batch size</b> 1 the indigo arrow is a violet one and can point
      almost the wrong way. Raise the batch and it swings into line, but slowly:
      the dashed ring around the teal tip is the spread of the average, and it
      shrinks like one over the square root, so sixteen times the work buys four
      times less noise. That poor exchange rate is the argument against very
      large batches. Push the slider to 256 and the batch is the whole set: the
      ring closes to nothing and the indigo arrow lies exactly on the teal one,
      the only batch size that is never wrong and never worth it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Let a run finish with a small batch. It does not stop at the minimum, it
      arrives and then jitters around it forever, in a ball whose radius the
      panel reports. Stochastic descent converges to a <b>region</b>, not a
      point, and the region is set by the learning rate against the batch size.
    </li>
    <li>
      That is why the two knobs move together. Double the batch and the noise
      falls by √2; double the learning rate as well and the ball is back to the
      size it was, which is the rule of thumb behind scaling the learning rate
      with the batch. The ball is also the useful part: a run that jitters
      cannot sit in a hole narrower than the jitter, so noise quietly selects
      the wide flat minima.
    </li>
  </Fragment>
</SimLayout>

<script>
  import { setupCanvas, makeText } from '../../../lib/sim';
  import { equation } from '../../../lib/equation';
  import { stageInk, arrow } from '../../../lib/draw';
  import { el, slider, check, transport, result, readout } from '../../../lib/panel';

  const TRUE = '#5eead4', BATCH = '#7c8cff', EX = '#a78bfa', PATH = '#f59e0b';
  const N = 256; // examples in the whole set

  const state = {
    batch: 8,
    lr: 0.3,
    cloud: true,
    singles: true,
    running: false,
    w: [0, 0],
    steps: 0,
    idx: [] as number[],
    hover: -1,
  };
  let path: number[][] = [];
  /** Errors of the draws taken at the current position, cleared whenever it moves. */
  let hist: { b: number; e: number }[] = [];

  const canvas = el('bn') as HTMLCanvasElement;
  const { ctx, W, H } = setupCanvas(canvas, () => { surfReady = false; draw(); });
  const { ink, inv } = stageInk(canvas);
  const { label } = makeText(ctx, ink);
  const setEq = equation();
  const setResult = result();
  const setRows = readout();

  function mulberry32(a: number) {
    return () => {
      a = (a + 0x6d2b79f5) | 0;
      let t = Math.imul(a ^ (a >>> 15), 1 | a);
      t = (t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), 61 | t)) ^ t;
      return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296;
    };
  }
  const rnd = mulberry32(20260906);
  const gauss = () => {
    const u = Math.max(1e-9, rnd());
    return Math.sqrt(-2 * Math.log(u)) * Math.cos(2 * Math.PI * rnd());
  };

  // A plain least-squares fit, so a per-example gradient is exact and cheap.
  // The two features have similar scales, so the examples point in every
  // direction and the fan of their gradients is worth looking at.
  const xs: number[][] = [], ys: number[] = [];
  const TRUE_W = [0.8, -0.4];
  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    const a = gauss() * 1.0, b = gauss() * 0.72;
    xs.push([a, b]);
    ys.push(TRUE_W[0] * a + TRUE_W[1] * b + gauss() * 0.55);
  }

  // The loss is exactly quadratic, so its value and its gradient are closed
  // forms in these five numbers rather than a sweep over the data.
  const [HA, HB, HC, D0, D1, E0] = (() => {
    let a = 0, b = 0, c = 0, d0 = 0, d1 = 0, e = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
      a += xs[i][0] * xs[i][0];
      b += xs[i][0] * xs[i][1];
      c += xs[i][1] * xs[i][1];
      d0 += ys[i] * xs[i][0];
      d1 += ys[i] * xs[i][1];
      e += ys[i] * ys[i];
    }
    return [a / N, b / N, c / N, d0 / N, d1 / N, e / (2 * N)];
  })();
  /** Where the whole set is happiest, the exact least-squares solution. */
  const WSTAR = (() => {
    const det = HA * HC - HB * HB;
    return [(HC * D0 - HB * D1) / det, (HA * D1 - HB * D0) / det];
  })();
  /** Far enough from the best fit to have a run to watch, and inside the map. */
  const START = [WSTAR[0] - 1.4, WSTAR[1] + 1.25];
  const loss = (w: number[]) =>
    0.5 * (HA * w[0] * w[0] + 2 * HB * w[0] * w[1] + HC * w[1] * w[1]) - (D0 * w[0] + D1 * w[1]) + E0;
  const gradFull = (w: number[]) => [HA * w[0] + HB * w[1] - D0, HB * w[0] + HC * w[1] - D1];

  /** How wrong the model is on example i, which sets the sign and size of its pull. */
  const resid = (i: number, w: number[]) => w[0] * xs[i][0] + w[1] * xs[i][1] - ys[i];
  function gradOne(i: number, w: number[]) {
    const r = resid(i, w);
    return [r * xs[i][0], r * xs[i][1]];
  }
  /** The gradient a given set of drawn examples reports. */
  function gradOf(idx: number[], w: number[]) {
    let g0 = 0, g1 = 0;
    for (const i of idx) {
      const r = resid(i, w);
      g0 += r * xs[i][0];
      g1 += r * xs[i][1];
    }
    return [g0 / idx.length, g1 / idx.length];
  }

  /** The spread of one example's gradient around the true one. */
  function sigmaAt(w: number[]) {
    const g = gradFull(w);
    let v = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
      const gi = gradOne(i, w);
      v += (gi[0] - g[0]) ** 2 + (gi[1] - g[1]) ** 2;
    }
    return Math.sqrt(v / N);
  }
  /**
   * Which shrinks like one over the square root once B of them are averaged,
   * and all the way to nothing when the batch is the whole set.
   */
  const shrink = (B: number) => Math.sqrt(Math.max(0, (N - B) / (N - 1)) / B);
  const noiseAt = (w: number[], B: number) => sigmaAt(w) * shrink(B);

  // --- the draw itself -------------------------------------------------------
  /** B distinct examples, the first B of a shuffle, so no example is used twice. */
  function pick(B: number, rand: () => number) {
    const bag = new Uint16Array(N);
    for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) bag[i] = i;
    const idx: number[] = new Array(B);
    for (let k = 0; k < B; k++) {
      const j = k + Math.floor(rand() * (N - k));
      const t = bag[k];
      bag[k] = bag[j];
      bag[j] = t;
      idx[k] = bag[k];
    }
    return idx;
  }
  function resample() {
    const B = state.batch;
    const idx = pick(B, rnd);
    state.idx = idx;
    const g = gradOf(idx, state.w), t = gradFull(state.w);
    hist.push({ b: B, e: Math.hypot(g[0] - t[0], g[1] - t[1]) });
    if (hist.length > 120) hist.shift();
  }

  // --- layout ----------------------------------------------------------------
  const DATA = { x: 76, y: 112, w: 312, h: 312 };
  const MAP = { x: 556, y: 112, w: 312, h: 312 };
  const PL = { x: 76, y: 528, w: 792, h: 148 };
  const RANGE = 2.2; // half-width of the parameter map, centred on the best fit
  const DR = 2.8; // half-width of the data panel

  const px = (w0: number) => MAP.x + ((w0 - WSTAR[0] + RANGE) / (2 * RANGE)) * MAP.w;
  const py = (w1: number) => MAP.y + MAP.h - ((w1 - WSTAR[1] + RANGE) / (2 * RANGE)) * MAP.h;
  const toW = (x: number, y: number) => [
    WSTAR[0] + ((x - MAP.x) / MAP.w) * 2 * RANGE - RANGE,
    WSTAR[1] + ((MAP.y + MAP.h - y) / MAP.h) * 2 * RANGE - RANGE,
  ];
  const dx = (v: number) => DATA.x + ((v + DR) / (2 * DR)) * DATA.w;
  const dy = (v: number) => DATA.y + DATA.h - ((v + DR) / (2 * DR)) * DATA.h;

  /** The two principal directions of the bowl, and how steep each one is. */
  const EIG = (() => {
    const tr = HA + HC, det = HA * HC - HB * HB;
    const disc = Math.sqrt(Math.max(0, (tr * tr) / 4 - det));
    const l1 = tr / 2 + disc, l2 = tr / 2 - disc;
    const v1 = Math.abs(HB) > 1e-12 ? [l1 - HC, HB] : [1, 0];
    const n1 = Math.hypot(v1[0], v1[1]) || 1;
    return { l1, l2, ang: Math.atan2(v1[1] / n1, v1[0] / n1) };
  })();

  // --- the loss surface, painted once and blitted afterwards -----------------
  const surf = document.createElement('canvas');
  let surfReady = false;
  function paintSurface() {
    surf.width = MAP.w * 2;
    surf.height = MAP.h * 2;
    const c = surf.getContext('2d')!;
    c.setTransform(2, 0, 0, 2, 0, 0);
    const STEP = 4;
    let lo = Infinity, hi = -Infinity;
    const grid: number[][] = [];
    for (let yy = 0; yy <= MAP.h; yy += STEP) {
      const row: number[] = [];
      for (let xx = 0; xx <= MAP.w; xx += STEP) {
        const v = Math.log(1e-3 + loss(toW(MAP.x + xx, MAP.y + yy)));
        row.push(v);
        lo = Math.min(lo, v);
        hi = Math.max(hi, v);
      }
      grid.push(row);
    }
    for (let r = 0; r < grid.length; r++)
      for (let k = 0; k < grid[r].length; k++) {
        const t = (grid[r][k] - lo) / Math.max(1e-9, hi - lo);
        c.fillStyle = `rgba(124,140,255,${0.03 + 0.3 * t})`;
        c.fillRect(k * STEP, r * STEP, STEP, STEP);
      }
    // the level sets themselves, drawn as the ellipses they are
    const sc = MAP.w / (2 * RANGE);
    c.save();
    c.beginPath();
    c.rect(0, 0, MAP.w, MAP.h);
    c.clip();
    for (let j = 1; j <= 9; j++) {
      const d = 2 * (0.02 * Math.pow(2.05, j));
      c.save();
      c.translate(px(WSTAR[0]) - MAP.x, py(WSTAR[1]) - MAP.y);
      c.rotate(-EIG.ang);
      c.beginPath();
      c.ellipse(0, 0, Math.sqrt(d / EIG.l1) * sc, Math.sqrt(d / EIG.l2) * sc, 0, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      c.restore();
      c.strokeStyle = ink(0.1 + (0.06 * (9 - j)) / 9);
      c.lineWidth = 1;
      c.stroke();
    }
    c.restore();
    surfReady = true;
  }

  function surface() {
    label('the loss over the two parameters', MAP.x, MAP.y - 34, 0.5, 'left', 13);
    label('what no single step ever sees', MAP.x, MAP.y - 16, 0.3, 'left', 11);
    if (!surfReady) paintSurface();
    ctx.drawImage(surf, MAP.x, MAP.y, MAP.w, MAP.h);
    ctx.strokeStyle = ink(0.14);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    ctx.strokeRect(MAP.x, MAP.y, MAP.w, MAP.h);
    ctx.strokeStyle = TRUE;
    ctx.lineWidth = 1.4;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.arc(px(WSTAR[0]), py(WSTAR[1]), 5, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    ctx.stroke();
    label('the best fit', px(WSTAR[0]) + 10, py(WSTAR[1]) + 4, 0.45, 'left', 11, TRUE);
    label('w₁', MAP.x + MAP.w, MAP.y + MAP.h + 20, 0.3, 'right', 11);
    label('w₂', MAP.x - 10, MAP.y + 10, 0.3, 'right', 11);
  }

  // --- the data, and which of it was drawn -----------------------------------
  /** Which examples turned up in the current draw. */
  function drawn() {
    const m = new Uint8Array(N);
    for (const i of state.idx) m[i] = 1;
    return m;
  }

  function dataPanel(m: Uint8Array) {
    label('the 256 examples', DATA.x, DATA.y - 34, 0.5, 'left', 13);
    label('the batch is the handful lit up', DATA.x, DATA.y - 16, 0.3, 'left', 11);
    ctx.strokeStyle = ink(0.14);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    ctx.strokeRect(DATA.x, DATA.y, DATA.w, DATA.h);
    ctx.strokeStyle = ink(0.09);
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(DATA.x, dy(0));
    ctx.lineTo(DATA.x + DATA.w, dy(0));
    ctx.moveTo(dx(0), DATA.y);
    ctx.lineTo(dx(0), DATA.y + DATA.h);
    ctx.stroke();

    ctx.save();
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.rect(DATA.x, DATA.y, DATA.w, DATA.h);
    ctx.clip();
    ctx.fillStyle = ink(0.2);
    for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
      if (m[i]) continue;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(dx(xs[i][0]), dy(xs[i][1]), 2.2, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.fill();
    }
    const showPull = state.idx.length <= 48;
    for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
      if (!m[i]) continue;
      const x = dx(xs[i][0]), y = dy(xs[i][1]);
      ctx.fillStyle = EX;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(x, y, 3.4, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.fill();
      if (!showPull) continue;
      // the direction this example pushes the parameters, which is its own
      // features, turned around when the model overshoots it
      const s = resid(i, state.w) >= 0 ? 1 : -1;
      const n = Math.hypot(xs[i][0], xs[i][1]) || 1;
      const ux = (s * xs[i][0]) / n, uy = (-s * xs[i][1]) / n;
      ctx.strokeStyle = `${EX}b0`;
      ctx.fillStyle = `${EX}b0`;
      ctx.lineWidth = 1.4;
      arrow(ctx, x + ux * 5, y + uy * 5, x + ux * 15, y + uy * 15, 4);
      ctx.fillStyle = EX;
    }
    if (state.hover >= 0) {
      const i = state.hover;
      ctx.strokeStyle = EX;
      ctx.lineWidth = 1.6;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(dx(xs[i][0]), dy(xs[i][1]), 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.stroke();
    }
    ctx.restore();

    label(
      `${state.idx.length} of the ${N} drawn at random`,
      DATA.x, DATA.y + DATA.h + 20, 0.45, 'left', 11, EX
    );
    label('x₁', DATA.x + DATA.w, DATA.y + DATA.h + 20, 0.3, 'right', 11);
    label('x₂', DATA.x - 10, DATA.y + 10, 0.3, 'right', 11);
  }

  /** The sentence that joins the two panels together. */
  function bridge() {
    const c = (DATA.x + DATA.w + MAP.x) / 2;
    label('every example', c, 214, 0.5, 'center', 11.5);
    label('you drew adds', c, 231, 0.5, 'center', 11.5);
    label('its own arrow', c, 248, 0.5, 'center', 11.5, EX);
    ctx.strokeStyle = ink(0.3);
    ctx.fillStyle = ink(0.3);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1.4;
    arrow(ctx, c - 58, 272, c + 58, 272, 7);
    label('their average', c, 302, 0.5, 'center', 11.5);
    label('is the step', c, 319, 0.5, 'center', 11.5);
    label('you take', c, 336, 0.5, 'center', 11.5, BATCH);
  }

  // --- the arrows on the surface ---------------------------------------------
  function arrows() {
    const w = state.w;
    const x = px(w[0]), y = py(w[1]);
    const g = gradFull(w);
    const gb = gradOf(state.idx, w);
    const sd = sigmaAt(w);
    // One example's gradient is drawn about 70px long whatever the position, so
    // the picture is always about the spread and never about the raw scale.
    const K = Math.min(70 / Math.max(1e-6, sd), 600);

    ctx.save();
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.rect(MAP.x, MAP.y, MAP.w, MAP.h);
    ctx.clip();

    if (state.cloud) {
      // the batches the same draw could just as well have produced
      const other = mulberry32(991);
      ctx.strokeStyle = `${BATCH}40`;
      ctx.fillStyle = `${BATCH}40`;
      ctx.lineWidth = 1.1;
      for (let k = 0; k < 32; k++) {
        const a = gradOf(pick(state.batch, other), w);
        arrow(ctx, x, y, x - a[0] * K, y + a[1] * K, 5);
      }
    }

    if (state.singles) {
      // at most a couple of dozen, or a large batch becomes a solid disc
      const shown = state.idx.slice(0, 24);
      ctx.strokeStyle = `${EX}66`;
      ctx.fillStyle = `${EX}66`;
      ctx.lineWidth = 1.2;
      for (const i of shown) {
        const gi = gradOne(i, w);
        arrow(ctx, x, y, x - gi[0] * K, y + gi[1] * K, 5);
      }
      if (state.idx.length > shown.length)
        label(`showing 24 of the ${state.idx.length}`, MAP.x + 8, MAP.y + MAP.h - 10, 0.35, 'left', 10.5, EX);
    }

    // the true gradient, and the ring the batch average lands inside
    ctx.strokeStyle = TRUE;
    ctx.fillStyle = TRUE;
    ctx.lineWidth = 2.6;
    arrow(ctx, x, y, x - g[0] * K, y + g[1] * K, 9);
    const ring = (sd / Math.sqrt(state.batch)) * K;
    if (ring > 1.5) {
      ctx.strokeStyle = `${BATCH}88`;
      ctx.setLineDash([3, 4]);
      ctx.lineWidth = 1.2;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(x - g[0] * K, y + g[1] * K, ring, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.stroke();
      ctx.setLineDash([]);
    }

    // the one example being hovered, picked out of the crowd
    if (state.hover >= 0) {
      const gi = gradOne(state.hover, w);
      ctx.strokeStyle = EX;
      ctx.fillStyle = EX;
      ctx.lineWidth = 2;
      arrow(ctx, x, y, x - gi[0] * K, y + gi[1] * K, 8);
    }

    // the batch average, the arrow the run actually walks along
    ctx.strokeStyle = BATCH;
    ctx.fillStyle = BATCH;
    ctx.lineWidth = 2.6;
    arrow(ctx, x, y, x - gb[0] * K, y + gb[1] * K, 9);
    ctx.restore();

    if (path.length > 1) {
      ctx.strokeStyle = PATH;
      ctx.lineWidth = 1.6;
      ctx.globalAlpha = 0.85;
      ctx.beginPath();
      path.forEach((p, i) => (i ? ctx.lineTo(px(p[0]), py(p[1])) : ctx.moveTo(px(p[0]), py(p[1]))));
      ctx.stroke();
      ctx.globalAlpha = 1;
    }
    ctx.fillStyle = PATH;
    ctx.strokeStyle = inv(0.75);
    ctx.lineWidth = 2;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.arc(x, y, 6, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    ctx.fill();
    ctx.stroke();

    // the ball the run ends up rattling around in
    const rad = ballRadius();
    const rr = (rad / (2 * RANGE)) * MAP.w;
    if (rr > 3) {
      ctx.strokeStyle = `${PATH}66`;
      ctx.setLineDash([3, 4]);
      ctx.lineWidth = 1.3;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(px(WSTAR[0]), py(WSTAR[1]), rr, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.stroke();
      ctx.setLineDash([]);
      label(
        'the ring is where it settles',
        MAP.x, MAP.y + MAP.h + 20, 0.45, 'left', 11.5, PATH
      );
    }
  }

  function noisePlot() {
    label('how far a draw misses', PL.x, PL.y - 34, 0.5, 'left', 13);
    label('the curve is the typical miss, the dots are the draws you took', PL.x, PL.y - 16, 0.3, 'left', 11);
    const LO = 1, HI = 256;
    const gx = (b: number) => PL.x + (Math.log(b / LO) / Math.log(HI / LO)) * PL.w;
    const s1 = sigmaAt(state.w) || 1;
    const gy = (v: number) => {
      const t = Math.log(Math.max(1e-9, v) / s1) / Math.log(1 / 20);
      return PL.y + Math.max(0, Math.min(1, t)) * PL.h;
    };
    ctx.strokeStyle = ink(0.12);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    ctx.strokeRect(PL.x, PL.y, PL.w, PL.h);

    // where the noise stops being smaller than the thing it is estimating
    const g = gradFull(state.w);
    const gm = Math.hypot(g[0], g[1]);
    if (gm < s1 && gm > s1 / 20) {
      ctx.strokeStyle = `${TRUE}99`;
      ctx.setLineDash([4, 4]);
      ctx.lineWidth = 1.2;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.moveTo(PL.x, gy(gm));
      ctx.lineTo(PL.x + PL.w, gy(gm));
      ctx.stroke();
      ctx.setLineDash([]);
      label('the true gradient itself', PL.x + PL.w - 10, gy(gm) - 7, 0.5, 'right', 11, TRUE);
    }

    ctx.strokeStyle = BATCH;
    ctx.lineWidth = 2.4;
    ctx.beginPath();
    for (let i = 0; i <= 80; i++) {
      const b = LO * (HI / LO) ** (i / 80);
      const x = gx(b), y = gy(s1 * shrink(b));
      i ? ctx.lineTo(x, y) : ctx.moveTo(x, y);
    }
    ctx.stroke();

    ctx.fillStyle = `${PATH}55`;
    for (const h of hist) {
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(gx(h.b), gy(h.e), 2.6, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.fill();
    }

    const x = gx(state.batch);
    ctx.strokeStyle = ink(0.18);
    ctx.setLineDash([3, 4]);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1.2;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(x, PL.y);
    ctx.lineTo(x, PL.y + PL.h);
    ctx.stroke();
    ctx.setLineDash([]);
    ctx.fillStyle = BATCH;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.arc(x, gy(s1 * shrink(state.batch)), 5, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    ctx.fill();
    const gb = gradOf(state.idx, state.w);
    ctx.fillStyle = PATH;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.arc(x, gy(Math.hypot(gb[0] - g[0], gb[1] - g[1])), 4.5, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    ctx.fill();

    for (const b of [1, 4, 16, 64, 256]) label(String(b), gx(b), PL.y + PL.h + 18, 0.3, 'center', 11);
    // the note hangs off the curve itself, so it never sits on top of it
    label('one over the square root', gx(1.3), gy(s1 * shrink(1.3)) + 58, 0.45, 'left', 11, BATCH);
    label('batch size', PL.x + PL.w, PL.y + PL.h + 36, 0.3, 'right', 11);
  }

  /** Where stochastic descent stops improving: noise times step, against curvature. */
  function ballRadius() {
    return state.lr * noiseAt(WSTAR, state.batch);
  }

  function draw() {
    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, W, H);
    dataPanel(drawn());
    bridge();
    surface();
    arrows();
    noisePlot();

    const g = gradFull(state.w);
    const gb = gradOf(state.idx, state.w);
    const gm = Math.hypot(g[0], g[1]);
    const bm = Math.hypot(gb[0], gb[1]);
    const err = Math.hypot(gb[0] - g[0], gb[1] - g[1]);
    const dot = (g[0] * gb[0] + g[1] * gb[1]) / Math.max(1e-12, gm * bm);
    const ang = (Math.acos(Math.max(-1, Math.min(1, dot))) * 180) / Math.PI;
    const n = noiseAt(state.w, state.batch);
    const rad = ballRadius();
    setEq({
      rule: [
        ['g_B = \\dfrac{1}{B}\\sum_{i \\in B} \\nabla L_i', BATCH],
        '\\qquad',
        ['\\mathbb{E}[g_B] = \\nabla L', TRUE],
        '\\qquad',
        ['\\operatorname{sd}[g_B] = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{B}}', PATH],
      ],
      live: [
        [`B = ${state.batch}`, BATCH],
        [`\\quad \\text{this draw is off by } ${ang.toFixed(0)}^\\circ`, EX],
        [`\\quad \\operatorname{sd}[g_B] = ${n.toFixed(4)}`, PATH],
        [`\\quad |\\nabla L| = ${gm.toFixed(4)}`, TRUE],
      ],
    });
    setRows({
      'r-drawn': `${state.idx.length} of ${N}`,
      'r-angle': `${ang.toFixed(1)}°`,
      'r-err': err.toFixed(4),
      'r-noise': n.toFixed(4),
      'r-true': gm.toFixed(4),
      'r-ratio': `${(n / Math.max(1e-9, gm)).toFixed(2)}×`,
      'r-ball': rad.toFixed(4),
      'r-steps': String(state.steps),
    });
    const ratio = n / Math.max(1e-9, gm);
    setResult({
      value: `${ang.toFixed(0)}° off`,
      colour: ratio > 1 ? PATH : TRUE,
      status: `${state.batch} example${state.batch > 1 ? 's' : ''} drawn · their average misses the true gradient by ${err.toFixed(3)}, and a typical draw misses by ${n.toFixed(3)} · that is ${ratio.toFixed(2)} times the gradient itself, and it settles into a ball of radius ${rad.toFixed(3)}`,
      note:
        ratio > 2
          ? 'The noise is larger than the gradient itself, so any single step points almost anywhere. The run still arrives, because the errors average out over many steps rather than within one.'
          : state.batch > 128
            ? 'A large batch buys a clean gradient at a poor exchange rate, and it removes the jitter that keeps a run out of narrow holes.'
            : 'Four times the batch halves the miss, never more, because the estimate improves with the square root of the count and not with the count.',
    });
  }

  // --- the run ---------------------------------------------------------------
  function step() {
    // the batch on screen is the one that gets used, then the next is drawn
    const g = gradOf(state.idx, state.w);
    state.w = [state.w[0] - state.lr * g[0], state.w[1] - state.lr * g[1]];
    path.push(state.w.slice());
    if (path.length > 600) path.shift();
    state.steps++;
    hist = []; // the errors were measured where the run no longer is
    resample();
  }
  function reset(at?: number[]) {
    state.w = at ?? START.slice();
    path = [state.w.slice()];
    state.steps = 0;
    hist = [];
    resample();
  }
  let last = 0;
  function frame(t: number) {
    if (!last) last = t;
    if (state.running && t - last > 28) {
      last = t;
      step();
    }
    draw();
    requestAnimationFrame(frame);
  }

  canvas.addEventListener('pointerdown', (e) => {
    const r = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
    const x = ((e.clientX - r.left) / r.width) * W;
    const y = ((e.clientY - r.top) / r.height) * H;
    if (x < MAP.x || x > MAP.x + MAP.w || y < MAP.y || y > MAP.y + MAP.h) return;
    const w = toW(x, y);
    reset([Math.max(-RANGE, Math.min(RANGE, w[0])), Math.max(-RANGE, Math.min(RANGE, w[1]))]);
  });

  canvas.addEventListener('pointermove', (e) => {
    const r = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
    const x = ((e.clientX - r.left) / r.width) * W;
    const y = ((e.clientY - r.top) / r.height) * H;
    let best = -1, bd = 12 * 12;
    if (x >= DATA.x && x <= DATA.x + DATA.w && y >= DATA.y && y <= DATA.y + DATA.h)
      for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
        const d = (dx(xs[i][0]) - x) ** 2 + (dy(xs[i][1]) - y) ** 2;
        if (d < bd) {
          bd = d;
          best = i;
        }
      }
    state.hover = best;
  });
  canvas.addEventListener('pointerleave', () => (state.hover = -1));

  slider('batch', (v) => {
    state.batch = v;
    hist = [];
    resample();
  });
  slider('lr', (v) => (state.lr = v / 100), (v) => (v / 100).toFixed(2));
  check('singles', (on) => (state.singles = on));
  check('cloud', (on) => (state.cloud = on));
  el('draw').addEventListener('click', () => resample());
  transport({
    onRun: (running) => (state.running = running),
    onStep: () => step(),
    onReset: () => reset(),
  });

  reset();
  requestAnimationFrame(frame);
</script>

<style>
  #bn {
    cursor: crosshair;
  }
</style>
